TEXT -96 -352 Left 2 ;Problem 2.4 - two masses, M1 pushed by the force f, coupled to M2 by a spring C1 || damper R1.
TEXT -96 -320 Left 2 ;Top: impedance analogy (the spring and damper share one relative velocity u1 - u2, so they are in SERIES here).  Bottom: mobility analogy.
TEXT -96 -272 Left 2 !.param f=1 Rm1=0.3 Mm1=0.001 Mm2=0.002 Cm1=0.01
TEXT 1024 -32 Left 2 ;u1 = I(Vu1),  u2 = I(Vu2)
TEXT 1024 0 Left 2 ;(L1, L2 have Rser=1u: V source + inductor loop)
TEXT 1024 528 Left 2 ;u1 = V(u1),  u2 = V(u2)
TEXT 1024 560 Left 2 ;mass = C to ground, spring || damper = L || 1/R in the link
TEXT -96 800 Left 2 !.ac dec 1000 1 10k
TEXT -96 848 Left 2 ;Plot I(Vu1), I(Vu2), V(u1), V(u2): u1 dips at 1/(2 pi sqrt(Mm2 Cm1)) = 35.6 Hz (M2 on the spring holds M1 still), u1 peaks at 71 Hz (reduced mass M1M2/(M1+M2) on C1 gives 61.6 Hz undamped; Q = 0.86 only, so the peak moves).
TEXT -96 880 Left 2 ;Limiting cases: set Mm1 or Mm2 = 1e6 (infinite mass), Cm1 = 1e-12 (rigid spring), Rm1 = 1e9 (rigid damper), Rm1 = 1e-9 with Cm1 = 1e6 (no coupling) and compare.
